# PATENT DISCLOSURE

## Title

**Imaging Apparatus, Image Sensor, and Image Processing Unit for Efficient Recognition Processing**

## Abstract

An imaging apparatus includes an image sensor and an image processing unit configured to reduce the computational load associated with recognition processing. The image sensor includes a pixel array, a readout circuit, and a sensor-side processing circuit. The sensor-side processing circuit generates recognition-assistance information from image signals obtained by the pixel array. The recognition-assistance information may include region-of-interest information, feature information, motion information, confidence information, or reduced-resolution image information. The image processing unit selects, based on the recognition-assistance information, one or more portions of the image signals and performs recognition processing on the selected portions. Readout resolution, frame rate, bit depth, exposure, and processing complexity may be controlled separately for respective spatial regions. Accordingly, unnecessary transfer and processing of image data are reduced while preserving image information relevant to recognition.

---

## Technical Field

The present disclosure relates to imaging technology and, more particularly, to an imaging apparatus, an image sensor, and an image processing unit configured to improve the efficiency of object recognition, classification, tracking, scene understanding, or other recognition processing.

---

## Background

Conventional imaging apparatuses generally read image signals from substantially all pixels of an image sensor and transfer full-resolution image data to an external image processor. The image processor then performs recognition processing, such as detecting or classifying an object, estimating a pose, identifying a person, recognizing text, or tracking movement.

This approach may consume substantial electrical power, memory bandwidth, and processing resources. In many scenes, however, only a limited portion of the captured image contains information useful for recognition. For example, a moving object may occupy only a small region of a surveillance image, or a road sign may occupy only a small region of an image captured by an automotive camera.

Although image data may be reduced before recognition processing, uniform reduction of resolution, frame rate, or bit depth can remove information needed for accurate recognition. There is therefore a need for an architecture that selectively preserves useful image information while reducing the transfer and processing of less useful information.

---

## Summary of the Disclosure

According to one aspect, an imaging apparatus includes:

1. an image sensor having a pixel array configured to generate image signals;
2. a sensor-side processing circuit configured to derive recognition-assistance information from at least a portion of the image signals; and
3. an image processing unit configured to determine, based on the recognition-assistance information, a processing condition for recognition processing and to perform the recognition processing under the determined processing condition.

The processing condition may specify one or more of:

- a spatial region to be processed;
- a resolution;
- a readout rate;
- a frame rate;
- a signal bit depth;
- a color component;
- an exposure condition;
- a recognition model;
- a layer or stage of a recognition model;
- a processing priority; or
- whether image data is to be retained, transmitted, or discarded.

According to another aspect, the image sensor supports region-dependent readout. A first region associated with a possible recognition target may be output at a first resolution, frame rate, or bit depth, while a second region may be output at a lower resolution, frame rate, or bit depth, or may be omitted.

According to another aspect, the sensor-side processing circuit generates compact recognition-assistance information without necessarily performing complete recognition. The recognition-assistance information can be generated using operations having lower computational complexity than the recognition processing performed by the image processing unit. Such operations may include temporal differencing, spatial filtering, edge detection, luminance statistics, color statistics, feature extraction, event detection, or low-complexity inference.

According to another aspect, the image processing unit uses the recognition-assistance information to select among multiple recognition paths. For example, a low-complexity recognition model may be used when confidence is high, and a higher-complexity recognition model may be used when confidence is low or when a candidate target satisfies a predetermined condition.

The disclosed configuration can reduce sensor readout power, data-transfer bandwidth, memory use, latency, and recognition-processing load.

---

## Brief Description of the Drawings

**FIG. 1** is a block diagram of an imaging apparatus according to an embodiment.

**FIG. 2** illustrates an image sensor having a pixel array divided into a plurality of controllable regions.

**FIG. 3** illustrates generation and use of recognition-assistance information.

**FIG. 4** illustrates region-dependent readout conditions.

**FIG. 5** is a flowchart of a recognition-processing method.

**FIG. 6** illustrates feedback from an image processing unit to an image sensor.

**FIG. 7** illustrates an implementation in which sensor-side and processor-side recognition stages form a cascaded recognition system.

---

## Detailed Description

### 1. Overall Configuration

Referring to FIG. 1, an imaging apparatus 100 includes an optical system 110, an image sensor 120, an image processing unit 140, a memory 150, and an output interface 160.

The optical system 110 forms an optical image of a scene on the image sensor 120. The optical system 110 may include one or more lenses, an aperture, an optical filter, a shutter, or an optical image stabilization mechanism.

The image sensor 120 includes:

- a pixel array 121;
- a row selection circuit 122;
- a column readout circuit 123;
- an analog-to-digital conversion circuit 124;
- a sensor-side processing circuit 125;
- a region control circuit 126; and
- a sensor interface 127.

The image processing unit 140 includes:

- an image acquisition circuit 141;
- a processing-condition determination circuit 142;
- a recognition circuit 143;
- a feedback control circuit 144; and
- an optional image reconstruction or enhancement circuit 145.

The components may be implemented in hardware, software executed by one or more processors, programmable logic, dedicated circuitry, or a combination thereof. The image sensor 120 and image processing unit 140 may be formed on separate semiconductor dies, stacked dies, or a common semiconductor substrate.

---

### 2. Pixel Array and Region-Based Operation

The pixel array 121 includes a plurality of photoelectric conversion elements arranged in rows and columns. The pixels may be visible-light pixels, infrared pixels, time-of-flight pixels, event-detection pixels, polarization-sensitive pixels, or combinations thereof.

The pixel array 121 is logically or physically divided into a plurality of regions. A region may correspond to:

- one pixel;
- a block of adjacent pixels;
- one or more rows or columns;
- a dynamically defined rectangular or nonrectangular area;
- a semantic region; or
- a region surrounding a detected feature or object candidate.

The region control circuit 126 independently controls a readout condition for respective regions. For example, a candidate region containing an object may be read at full resolution and twelve-bit depth, while a background region may be read using pixel binning and eight-bit depth. A region having no detected change may be read less frequently or not read during a given frame period.

The region control circuit 126 may also independently control exposure duration, analog gain, conversion gain, sampling frequency, or the number of accumulated frames. Thus, image quality can be concentrated in regions expected to be useful for recognition.

---

### 3. Recognition-Assistance Information

The sensor-side processing circuit 125 receives digital or analog image signals and generates recognition-assistance information. The recognition-assistance information is information usable to determine where, when, or how recognition processing should be performed.

Examples include:

- coordinates or boundaries of a candidate region;
- a motion map;
- an edge map;
- a saliency map;
- a luminance or color histogram;
- a texture descriptor;
- a feature vector;
- a feature map;
- a depth estimate;
- an event location and timestamp;
- an object-presence score;
- an object-category candidate;
- a confidence value;
- an image-quality value;
- an occlusion value; or
- a change value relative to an earlier frame.

The sensor-side processing circuit 125 may calculate temporal differences between a current signal and a stored reference signal. A pixel block having a temporal difference greater than a threshold may be designated as an active block. Adjacent active blocks may be grouped into a candidate region.

Alternatively, the sensor-side processing circuit 125 may apply one or more convolution filters to generate a low-level feature map. The feature map can be transmitted instead of, or together with, image data. In an embodiment, initial layers of a neural network are executed by the sensor-side processing circuit 125, and subsequent layers are executed by the recognition circuit 143.

Recognition-assistance information may be associated with a timestamp, frame identifier, exposure identifier, region identifier, or sensor identifier so that the image processing unit 140 can associate the information with corresponding image signals.

---

### 4. Selective Image Readout

The image sensor 120 may operate in at least two readout stages.

During a first stage, the sensor reads reduced image information from a relatively large field of view. The reduced image information may have reduced resolution, reduced bit depth, or reduced frame frequency. The sensor-side processing circuit 125 uses the reduced image information to identify one or more candidate regions.

During a second stage, the image sensor reads image information from the candidate regions under a higher-information readout condition. The second-stage readout may occur in the same frame period, a subsequent frame period, or asynchronously in response to an event.

For example:

- the entire pixel array may be read at one-quarter resolution;
- a candidate region may then be read at full resolution;
- a surrounding context region may be read at intermediate resolution; and
- the remaining region may not be transferred.

The resulting output can include a region table identifying the location, dimensions, resolution, bit depth, and timing of each output region.

This operation reduces data volume while allowing detailed recognition of a candidate target.

---

### 5. Image Processing Unit

The image acquisition circuit 141 receives image data and recognition-assistance information from the image sensor 120.

The processing-condition determination circuit 142 determines how the recognition circuit 143 is to process the received data. The determination may be based on:

- candidate-region size or location;
- motion speed;
- feature strength;
- sensor-side confidence;
- available power;
- available memory bandwidth;
- processor utilization;
- application priority;
- environmental conditions; or
- a previously generated recognition result.

The recognition circuit 143 may perform object detection, object classification, biometric recognition, optical character recognition, pose estimation, semantic segmentation, behavior recognition, or tracking.

The recognition circuit 143 may include multiple recognition models having different complexity or accuracy. The processing-condition determination circuit 142 can select an appropriate model. For example, a compact model may process an initial candidate, and a larger model may be invoked only when the compact model produces a confidence value within an uncertain range.

The recognition circuit 143 may process only image data corresponding to candidate regions. In some embodiments, image data outside the candidate regions is used only to provide contextual information or is omitted from recognition processing.

---

### 6. Cascaded Recognition

In an embodiment, recognition processing is divided between the image sensor 120 and image processing unit 140.

The sensor-side processing circuit 125 performs a first recognition stage having relatively low computational complexity. The first stage may determine whether a region has a likelihood of containing a recognition target.

The recognition circuit 143 performs a second recognition stage on selected regions. The second stage may use a more complex neural network or another recognition algorithm to identify or classify the target.

A sensor-side confidence value may control whether the second stage is performed. For example:

- when the confidence value is below a first threshold, the region may be discarded;
- when the confidence value is between the first and second thresholds, a high-accuracy model may be used;
- when the confidence value exceeds the second threshold, a lower-complexity confirmation model may be used.

This conditional execution reduces unnecessary use of the higher-complexity recognition model.

---

### 7. Feedback Control

The feedback control circuit 144 generates control information based on a recognition result. The control information is transmitted to the image sensor 120 and may specify:

- a region to be tracked;
- a desired region size;
- a frame rate;
- an exposure time;
- a gain;
- a focus setting;
- a readout resolution;
- a bit depth;
- a feature type to be generated; or
- a duration for which a region is to remain active.

For example, after a pedestrian is detected, the image processing unit 140 predicts the pedestrian’s location in a subsequent frame. The predicted region is supplied to the image sensor 120, which reads the predicted region at a higher frame rate than the remaining image area.

If the tracked target is lost, the image sensor 120 may return to a wider low-resolution search mode. Thus, the apparatus can alternate between a search state and a target-tracking state.

---

### 8. Recognition-Aware Data Format

The sensor interface 127 may output data packets containing both image information and control metadata. A packet may include:

- a packet header;
- a frame or event timestamp;
- region coordinates;
- a readout-condition identifier;
- pixel or feature data;
- a candidate class;
- a confidence value; and
- an error-detection code.

Different packets associated with the same frame may represent different regions at different resolutions or bit depths. The image acquisition circuit 141 interprets the metadata and maps the received data to corresponding locations or recognition tasks.

Where a human-viewable image is also required, the image reconstruction circuit 145 may combine the differently sampled regions into a display image. Recognition processing, however, need not wait for reconstruction of the complete image.

---

### 9. Example Operation

FIG. 5 illustrates an example method.

At step S101, the pixel array 121 captures image signals.

At step S102, reduced or preliminary image signals are read by the sensor-side processing circuit 125.

At step S103, the sensor-side processing circuit 125 extracts recognition-assistance information and identifies one or more candidate regions.

At step S104, the region control circuit 126 determines readout conditions for the candidate and noncandidate regions.

At step S105, selected image data and corresponding recognition-assistance information are transferred to the image processing unit 140.

At step S106, the processing-condition determination circuit 142 selects a recognition region, recognition model, and processing priority.

At step S107, the recognition circuit 143 performs recognition processing on the selected data.

At step S108, the feedback control circuit 144 determines whether updated sensor control is required.

At step S109, when updated control is required, a control command is supplied to the image sensor 120 for a subsequent capture or readout operation.

The order of the steps may be changed, and multiple steps may be executed concurrently or asynchronously.

---

### 10. Example Applications

The disclosed apparatus may be incorporated into:

- a digital camera;
- a mobile terminal;
- a surveillance camera;
- a vehicle-mounted camera;
- a robot;
- an industrial inspection system;
- a medical imaging system;
- an augmented-reality device;
- an unmanned aerial vehicle; or
- an Internet-of-Things device.

In a vehicle application, candidate regions may correspond to pedestrians, vehicles, traffic signals, lane markings, or road signs. In a surveillance application, high-resolution readout may be initiated only in regions exhibiting motion. In an industrial application, recognition-assistance information may identify a region containing a potential defect.

---

## Technical Effects

The disclosed technology may provide one or more of the following effects:

1. reduced transfer of image data from the image sensor;
2. reduced memory bandwidth and storage requirements;
3. reduced power consumption by the sensor and image processor;
4. reduced recognition latency;
5. preservation of high-resolution information in relevant regions;
6. adaptive allocation of processing resources;
7. improved tracking through sensor feedback; and
8. scalable operation under changing power or processing constraints.

These effects may be obtained without requiring full semantic recognition within the image sensor.

---

## Exemplary Claims

### Claim 1 — Imaging Apparatus

An imaging apparatus comprising:

- an image sensor including a pixel array configured to generate image signals and a sensor-side processing circuit configured to generate recognition-assistance information from at least a portion of the image signals; and
- an image processing unit configured to:
  - determine, based on the recognition-assistance information, a processing condition for recognition processing; and
  - perform the recognition processing on selected image data under the determined processing condition,

wherein the processing condition identifies at least one of a spatial region, a resolution, a frame rate, a bit depth, a processing priority, or a recognition model.

### Claim 2

The imaging apparatus of claim 1, wherein the image sensor is configured to read a first region of the pixel array under a first readout condition and a second region under a second readout condition different from the first readout condition.

### Claim 3

The imaging apparatus of claim 2, wherein the first region is identified by the recognition-assistance information as containing a candidate recognition target, and wherein the first readout condition provides a higher spatial resolution, higher frame rate, or greater bit depth than the second readout condition.

### Claim 4

The imaging apparatus of claim 1, wherein the recognition-assistance information includes at least one of motion information, edge information, saliency information, a feature map, a candidate-region location, an object-presence score, or a confidence value.

### Claim 5

The imaging apparatus of claim 1, wherein the sensor-side processing circuit performs a first stage of a recognition model and the image processing unit performs a second stage of the recognition model using an output of the first stage.

### Claim 6

The imaging apparatus of claim 1, wherein the image processing unit is configured to select, based on a confidence value included in the recognition-assistance information, one of a plurality of recognition models having different computational complexities.

### Claim 7

The imaging apparatus of claim 1, wherein the image processing unit is configured to transmit feedback information to the image sensor based on a recognition result, and wherein the image sensor changes a readout condition of a region identified by the feedback information.

### Claim 8 — Image Sensor

An image sensor comprising:

- a pixel array including a plurality of pixel regions;
- a readout circuit configured to read image signals from the plurality of pixel regions;
- a sensor-side processing circuit configured to derive recognition-assistance information from the image signals; and
- a region control circuit configured to set different readout conditions for respective pixel regions based on the recognition-assistance information.

### Claim 9

The image sensor of claim 8, wherein the sensor-side processing circuit identifies a candidate region using reduced-resolution image signals and causes the readout circuit to read the candidate region at a higher resolution.

### Claim 10

The image sensor of claim 8, wherein the sensor-side processing circuit outputs a data packet containing region-identification information and at least one of pixel data, feature data, or confidence information.

### Claim 11 — Image Processing Unit

An image processing unit comprising:

- an acquisition circuit configured to receive image data and recognition-assistance information generated by an image sensor;
- a processing-condition determination circuit configured to select a portion of the image data based on the recognition-assistance information; and
- a recognition circuit configured to perform recognition processing on the selected portion while omitting or reducing recognition processing for another portion of the image data.

### Claim 12

The image processing unit of claim 11, further comprising a feedback control circuit configured to provide the image sensor with a command specifying at least one of a target region, a readout resolution, a frame rate, an exposure condition, or a bit depth.

### Claim 13 — Method

A method of operating an imaging apparatus, the method comprising:

1. generating image signals using a pixel array;
2. generating recognition-assistance information from at least a portion of the image signals;
3. selecting, based on the recognition-assistance information, image data corresponding to a candidate region;
4. determining a recognition-processing condition for the selected image data; and
5. performing recognition processing on the selected image data under the determined recognition-processing condition.

### Claim 14

The method of claim 13, further comprising changing a readout condition of the candidate region based on a result of the recognition processing.

### Claim 15 — Non-Transitory Medium

A non-transitory computer-readable medium storing instructions that, when executed by one or more processors of an image processing unit, cause the one or more processors to:

- receive image data and recognition-assistance information from an image sensor;
- select a spatial portion of the image data based on the recognition-assistance information;
- select one of a plurality of recognition-processing paths for the spatial portion; and
- perform recognition processing using the selected recognition-processing path.

---

## Conclusion

The disclosed imaging apparatus distributes preliminary analysis, selective readout, and recognition processing across an image sensor and an image processing unit. By identifying potentially relevant information before transferring or fully processing an image, the apparatus can allocate sensing, communication, and computing resources to regions and times having greater recognition value.
